What I Did

Completed the Microsoft Learn module Introduction to retrieval-augmented generation concepts and passed the module assessment.

The module introduced how Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) can help generative AI applications produce responses grounded in relevant information.

What I Learned

I learned the purpose of RAG and the general process used to provide an AI model with relevant information before it generates a response.

The process can be understood as:

1. Prepare the data so it can be searched and retrieved effectively.
2. Retrieve relevant information based on the user’s request.
3. Provide the retrieved information to the generative AI model.
4. Generate a grounded response using the retrieved context.
5. Evaluate and improve the solution to make retrieval and responses more effective.

Key Takeaways

The main concept I took from this module is that RAG helps connect generative AI with relevant external information.

Instead of relying only on what the model already knows, a RAG system can retrieve relevant information and use that information as context when generating its response.

I also learned that building a RAG solution isn’t only about retrieval. The preparation of the data and the evaluation of the overall system are also important.

Practical Relevance

RAG is particularly relevant to business AI applications where responses need to be grounded in specific organisational or domain information.

For example, a company could use a RAG-based application to help users find and answer questions about internal documentation, provided the system is designed with appropriate data access and security controls.

